    "payload": {
      "feedback": "The response provides a solid high\u2011level overview (value proposition, personas, features, and a few user stories) but falls short of being a complete, actionable product spec. Missing or weak areas include:\n1. **Detailed Requirements**: No functional requirements, acceptance criteria, or edge\u2011case handling (e.g., what happens when a vet\u2019s schedule system is offline, how cancellations are processed, or how to handle multiple pets per user).\n2. **Non\u2011Functional Requirements**: No mention of performance (response time for real\u2011time availability), scalability, security (HIPAA\u2011like data protection for pet health info), privacy, accessibility, or offline support.\n3. **Technical Architecture**: Absent are any diagrams or description of system components (mobile client, backend services, third\u2011party scheduling API integrations, geolocation service, notification service, database schema, etc.).\n4. **Data Model**: No entities defined (User, Pet, Clinic, Appointment, Review, Availability Slot) or relationships, which are needed for developers to start implementation.\n5. **User Experience Details**: Wireframe references, navigation flow, error states, loading indicators, and UI copy are not provided.\n6. **Prioritization & Roadmap**: While feature priority numbers are listed, there is no rationale, MVP definition, or phased rollout plan.\n7. **Metrics & Success Criteria**: No KPIs (e.g., time to book, user retention, booking conversion rate) or analytics requirements.\n8. **Regulatory/Legal Considerations**: Missing consent handling for location data, payment processing compliance, and any veterinary licensing constraints.\n9. **Testing & Validation**: No test cases, quality\u2011assurance strategy, or beta\u2011testing plan.\n10. **Monetization & Business Model**: No discussion of revenue streams (subscription, commission, ads) or cost structure.\nTo be actionable, the spec must flesh out these gaps with concrete, testable requirements, architecture diagrams, data schemas, UI mockups, and a clear MVP roadmap. Without them, developers cannot reliably estimate effort or begin implementation.",
      "idea": "A mobile app that helps dog owners find nearby vets with real-time availability",
      "focus": "Define user personas (dog owners, vets), outline core features (location-based search, real-time availability, appointment booking, reviews, push notifications), create wireframes and user journey maps for the mobile app, specify vet verification process and data privacy requirements, and draft a product requirement document with MVP scope."
    },
    "timestamp": "2026-04-21T08:38:29.989687+00:00",
    "parent_message_id": "msg-6c3914b3"
